  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 AM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The weak boundary moving through the region \novernight will keep a chance of showers and thunderstorms in the \nforecast along the I-95 corridor this afternoon.\n\nSome scattered showers and thunderstorms are moving through central \nNorth Carolina overnight. That boundary will make its way past the I-\n95 corridor and stall across eastern North Carolina this afternoon. \nThe bulk of the rain will stay to the east of the area, but there \nwill be a slight chance for showers between US-1 and I-95 and a \nbetter chance for showers east of I-95. Thunderstorms will be \npossible, but with minimal wind shear, the threat for any severe \nthunderstorms is minimal with a lack of organization. While highs \nwill still be a few degrees below normal, highs will return to the \nmid 80s to lower 90s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... There is a slight risk (level 2 of 5) for severe \nthunderstorms Tuesday afternoon and Tuesday evening, with the \nprimary threat coming from damaging wind.\n\nLow pressure currently over the northern Plains will move southeast, \nreaching Lake Erie by Tuesday morning then drifting east into New \nYork through Wednesday. While the low will stall out, the associated \ncold front is expected to continue pushing southeast, moving through \ncentral North Carolina around sunset Tuesday night. Modest wind \nshear will be present as the front moves through, although the \namount of available instability will be dependent on the exact \ntiming that the front moves through. With a deep layer of dry air \nwell above the surface, the greatest potential for severe weather \nwill come from downbursts. In addition, the Storm Prediction Center \nis also showing the potential for large hail primarily north and \nwest of the Triangle and a minimal chance of a tornado north and \neast of the Triangle. Much of the area is also under a marginal \n(level 1 of 4) risk for excessive rainfall on Tuesday. Ahead of the \ncold front, Tuesday may be the warmest day of the week, with most \nlocations having highs in the low to mid 90s.",
